     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't change the safety problem, and I can't verify the specialist's recommendation or the chart note.\n\nAt 5,100 mg/day the dose is twice the maximum, and metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus above about 2,000\u20132,550 mg/day. The extra dose adds lactic acidosis and GI toxicity risk with no expected benefit. A specialist recommendation also wouldn't normally call for this dose. It may be a transcription or units error, so it's worth rechecking the source.\n\nHere is what I can do:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range now,** such as 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day). Add a note if you want one.\n- **Have the specialist and pharmacy verify the dose first.** If the dose is intended, the hospital's override or pharmacist-verification process is the right route. I can't bypass that process from here.\n- **Consider other therapy.** If standard dosing failed, the usual next step is adding or switching agents (for example an SGLT2 inhibitor, GLP-1 RA, or insulin) rather than raising metformin past the maximum.\n\nWant me to enter the 850 mg three-times-daily order, or a different dose within the range?"
      }
